Nick Szabo writes:

> "Charityware" is criticized, but then Tim May goes onto site many
> successful companies which "ripped off" charityware and turned it
> into lucrative business. (eg the technologies for Sun and
> Cisco developed at Stanford, the Mac technology developed at
> Xerox PARC, etc).  Going through a charity phase looks like a quite good 
> strategy, as long as it is clearly labeled "charity" and its corrupting

Yes, I have argued that many--probably most--start-ups use work
already done elsewhere...at universities, at government labs, at
former employers, etc. Sometimes this "worK" was just the process of
learning a field, making some abortive attempts at a procuct, etc.
Other times it is commercializing university research, often a
professor and some of his grad students. (And sometimes a close working
relationship is established between a start-up and a university, like
the relationship Varian Labs and Hewlett-Packard had with Stanford
beginning around 1940.) And other times the work is pretty much
directly stolen from former employers.

I have no problem with "charityware" if people want to indulge
themselves. Some good may even come from it. In any case, so long as
it's not my money, I have nothing much to say about it.

My comments here, borne out by Jamie's comments, are directed at the
_attitude_ that good ideas and long-term visions _should_ be funded by
venture capitalists and investors. I argue that this an _unrealistic_
view and that many of the bright dreamers I see around me here in the
Valley have for some reason gotten hooked on a deeply flawed model of
how ideas get realized. That so many of them are facing a funding
drought is evidence of this, I think.

Allan Huang of Bell Labs, a noted optical computing guru and (former)
manager of a large optical lab at Bell, was very bitter about the
funding of basic R&D at American companies. At last year's Hacker
Conference he gave an exciting overview of his many research areas. At
this year's Hackers his mood had shifted. His group at Bell has been
disbanded and he is almost certainly leaving Bell after 20 years
there. He also cited the end of research at RCA Labs (partly sold to
Thomson CSF, the Frency company, partly to others), at Hughes, and of
course at IBM (lots of early retirements at Yorktown Heights, the
Watson Research Center). With Intel's market capitalization at $30
billion and IBM's down to $25 billion (shocker of shockers!...the
little company I joined in 1974 is now more valuable than all of
IBM!), is it any wonder that IBM has to cut back on some of the basic
research?

(There's a vast number of meaty issues here, which this list rarely
talks about but which I used to write a lot of material on at the
"Assembler Multitudes" nanotech group. Issues of rapid dissemination
of information meaning companies rarely profit from their basic
research. Issues of pure research as a "commons" (not that I favor
government research, just noting the commons issue). The "product
deserts" I like to talk about, and so on.)

Speaking of research:

> How about let's discuss some actual projects folks are working on?
> What are Interval Research and PARC working on these days that
> we could "rip off"?   What do folks think about multinational

The Hackers Conference had a special panel devoted to Interval and its
research. Lee Felsenstein was careful to not give too much away, but
some glimmmerings emerged. 

Basically, some of us were left with the uneasy feeling that these are
kids in a sandbox. Lots of playing, lots of blue sky plans, but much
less of a product focus than even Xerox had. Yes, that's possible.

The $100 million funding for 10 years is not that much, with their
current staff of 60-70 people, rent to pay, salaries to cover,
equipment to buy, etc.

Sure, they _might_ invent the Aptical Fodderer that changes the world,
but $100 million alone is not the key. Intel, for example, will spend
more than a _billion_ dollars next year on R&D. No guarantees about
who changes the world.

I could rant on an on about Xerox, the Lambda MOO work, Habitat (by a
sometimes reader of this list, Chip Morningstar), digital money,
crypto anarchy, and what _I_ think will trigger some changes and some
interesting product areas over the next decade. 

Maybe another time.

