From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Shower products.

Charlie Stross writes:
>Similar flow-through water heaters are common in the UK, and
>you get used to them after a bit. The problem is if you open
>up the tap the water cools down; you get a constant energy input
>from the heater, but increased volume of water through it.

I think two types of instant water heater are being confused here: electric
and gas.  Back-of-an-envelope calculations indicate that the electricity
supply is incapable of heating more than a feeble trickle to a useful
temperature, as is confirmed by their performance in practice. 
Gas-operated heaters, however, can easily provide a good flow of water
hotter than you can put your hand in.  They're common in the UK, especially
in houses that have been converted into multiple apartments, as it's the
easiest way to provide a hot water supply in rooms that weren't originally
designed for it.  They can supply water as fast as you would get it from a
hot water tank, and have the major advantage that the hot water runs as
long as the tap's open.  No tank to run cold, no heater to remember to turn
on in advance or turn off when the house is empty, just fit-and-forget
simplicity.

All tools should be as simple (he says, grasping for extropian relevance).

