Yep.

You want elegance?

Try this. Put a pressure-operated valve on the cold water pipe leading
into the hot water tank, so it doesn't begin to refill until you pull
the plug on the bath (or the sink). Wrap the cold water inlet pipe
around the drain with a convoluted, thin radiative surface between
them. Cold water comes in at the bottom (near the external drain) and
is pumped past progressively warmer water on the way up into the tank.
This is called a countercurrent heat exchanger. Design it well enough
and you'll recover most of the energy from the draining bathwater.

Manufacturing-wise, you'd have to figure out how to market a much more
complex bath drainage pipe mechanism. But it shouldn't be too hard, in
practice -- there's still only one inlet into the hot water tank, you
can put the inlet pressure valve at the bottom of the drain, and keep
the entire mechanism down to a single (albeit complex) piece of
two-duct tubing.


