Cheap, DIY solid-volume renderer (3D printer, CAM lathe, general
purpose robot arm with assembler fittings) capable of making and
fitting together molded (soft material) and machined (hard metal)
parts. Smaller and cheaper than a compact car, so you can keep it in a
garage (or the living room -- shades of SF fans with heavy-duty
stencil duplicators :).

When that happens, forget gun control (as has been observed) --
the main problem will be procuring detonators and explosives for
ammunition.  You can also rate this as the beginning of the end
for the "consumer society", at least in terms of consuming
physical products -- everything will be back to bespoke, individually
reworked designs made in small workshops to the precision standards
currently associated with production lines.

Second gadget: high bandwidth bidirectional comms to the home -- ATM,
TCP/IP-2 over ATM, that kind of thing. The Internet as the dominant
political entity in the developed world.

(Crypto anarchy is one possible consequence of this. So is a tyranny
as total as anything Hitler tried to achieve in Germany. Intermediate
political equilibria currently seem unlikely, however desirable a
comfortable compromise might be.)

Third gadget: the domestic STM or early nanoscale engineering
facility. Chip designs on disk. Build your own electronic controllers
for the macro tools you fabricate on the lathe.

Note that gadgets 1 and 3 will mostly be applied to engineering shops
rather than as consumer goods; not many people are into that kind of
engineering. But the consequences will be radical -- the era of mass
production will grind to dust on a million back-yard manufacturing
systems.

Fourth gadget (probably dependent on all of these): cheap, easy and
out of control space probes. See the recent discussion on sci.space of
what you can put into interplanetary space on the back of a pegasus
launcher, or Bruce Sterling's recent article (F&SF, WER's gopher) on
microprobes, virtual reality, and exploring the outer solar system
from the comfort of your own home.

Fifth gadget: totally soft consumer durables. There already exist top
end cameras, synthesizers, and stereos with RS-232 ports. How long
before _everything_ gets a common smart interface and programmability?
Like your kitchen toaster, so you can dial in a better browning
algorithm? (Okay, so that's an exaggeration :-)

Sixth gadget: I've already posted here about my ideal PDA, so I won't
repeat myself.

Seventh gadget: intelligent multimedia products.  Not just
encyclopaedias, but "packet professionals"; law-in-a-box, investment
advisor, family practitioner, and so on.  There are probably legal
liability questions to be resolved before we see expert decision-support
systems being marketed to the public, but imagine (for a moment) a
package you load into your PDA that has access to the sum total of your
chosen country or PPL's body of case law, and that can make fairly basic
inferences on the basis of that body and questions you put to it.  Not
as good or as creative as a real professional adviser, but enough of a
prop to ensure that _everybody_ can exercise the level of insight of an
"educated layperson" instead of an ignorant barbarian wondering at the
guild secrets.

Eight gadget: I don't know. You don't know. But the odds are there
_will_ be an eighth gadget, and it will be miraculous, and none of us
understand why we didn't forsee it or how we could live without it
before. 



-- Charlie
