> My hunch is that the heat loss in a porcelein tub is much faster, 
> based on having used a plastic tub and noticing that the water seemed
> to stay hot a much longer time. 

Actually, it is just the opposite. Heat loss through a porcelain tub
is much less than through a plastic tub. The reason it seemed like the
water stayed hot a much longer time is that you probably neglected
to draw your bath properly. If you just fill up the tub and climb
in, the large mass of cold porcelain will suck up all the heat, and
the water will be tepid in no time, where as the plastic tub, having much
less mass, will take less heat out of the water. 

The proper way to bathe is:

1. Fill the tub with scalding hot water. (Or better, have someone else
   draw the bath for you, while you lounge around waiting).
2. Allow the tub itself to heat up. This may take 5 or 10 minutes,
   depending on the temperature and mass of the tub.
3. Correct the temperature in the tub. If you are lucky, it needs 
   some cold water.
4. Climb in and soak.

When done properly, you will find yourself luxuriating in water precisely
the correct temperature, in a tub that is a bit warmer. As the water
loses heat from its surface, it will GAIN heat from the warmer tub, and
you can soak until your skin wrinkles. Method is hopelessly politically 
incorrect, but absolutely extropianly correct.

I am boundlessly optimistic that I may someday soon have a true bath room,
with a tub large enough to really enjoy.

> Ok, what kind of a patch would you use to salvage the heat from an
> efficient tub when you're done with the bath? 

Just leave the water in the tub until it is the same temperature
as the air in the room. I am shocked at how little people on the
list actually know about the essentials of bathing!

> though I think you'd want the slow drain
> feature to be optional in case someone else wants a bath right away.

If someone is in a hurry, they don't want a bath, they want a shower.

