In <9312031506.AA03115@ramsesii.ultryx.com>, James E. Hoburg writes:
|> David Friedman writes:
|> > Consider an n-dimensional space, representing characteristics of books. No
|> > assumptions are made initially about what characteristics, or even what
|> > sort of characteristics, each dimension represents. A reader's ideal book
|> > is a point somewhere in the space. A book is a point somewhere in the
|> > space. 
|> 
|> How would such filtering mechanisms work in the  face of  shifting tastes or
|> viewpoints?
|> 
|> Since the ignition  of the information big-bang, it seems that such  filters
|> are ever more valuable just  to winnow signal from noise.   However, I can't
|> help but notice  how frequently people tend to read (accept input) only that
|> which confirms their currently held beliefs and views.  Wouldn't use of such
|> filters  increase  an  already  present  tendency  to  wander  into  memetic
|> cul-de-sacs?


The fit-to-current-fact of such a mechanism will we hard to quantify,
but intuitively it depends on n, on the size of the population of
reader/reviewers, and on the diversity of their tastes. So, the
overall effectiveness of the system could be independent of individual
changes in taste, and how well it accommodates changes in taste would
depend on the frequency of the individual's resort to the system.

It's possible to set up the dimensions so that they are somewhat
polemically blind, that is, there's a way for a reviewer to express
his opinion of the quality of the logic and rhetoric in a book, but
not whether he agrees with the conclusion. This might be frustrating
to the zealot!

Over time, you could see how well you tracked with specific other
readers, also, which would be sort of like the way we choose what
critics to listen to today, but with more data to confirm our guesses.
(I apologize to DDFr for not recalling whether he covered this already)

Since the model is set up in terms of vector spaces, you can have fun
picking out a set of m < n of your favorite or least favorite books
and seeing what subspaces they generate, and kicking out a list of
the books in each one; or looking to see if you have m >= n books that
span 80 percent or more of your favorites.

Wines are sometimes rated using a simpleminded version of this scheme,
but most often information is thrown away by scalarizing to a 20-point
or 100-point scale, whereupon you have only your assessment of the
past performance of the rater to go on.

-- Craig


